#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119561 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "6a082146-0d63-59dd-9224-a319d73ca59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4827" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/acda5be1511b340b3a947cbaa4dedfd7a3175029d2937481b261c7a0c4eb5348.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -\\mathbf{b}_2$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = -3\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/ac75a1bca2b8012016657f4d1d88fdf1725c84b2a3ec48376bbcb4670f134ba3.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{b}_{2})-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/d98192a7e16e605311ee2bb8791e6d86005c6d565ede80e68aaaeaf8097721a2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119562 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "f07356b8-aeb3-50ac-b017-c370d87395d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4828" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/1e4b0e4345ef6d0bd0b350cc973eaa7c15b654e76b06cd6f42740af0ab8fc877.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/a5e125038beb90ff4fd2b16bea5b08f83855c80f2a70a73ccfc15fa53fd45a30.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2,$ and $\\mathbf c_3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3{b}_{2})-5(-{b}_{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/f78654ee0612c6daa7e7a1fa26fb56ce0c3c94f6a7ba29805a569eac643946eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119563 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "91fac4cd-7097-5244-a385-9f3f76395912" :question/math-academy-id "e-4829" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/aed9216ce4c536aad07f26eec0e0cb7e3032147a19c13e0a2d42925083c49518.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left( -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/80531bde3f2ebb6314a3a297efa44e3e21a33d0b5f628c67c39ef0fa288f4c78.png) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-3(-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+6{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/f678c1ecc9878c30b04e891af9e6933f1edb42af8e155c3f4719838210e1cc1d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1f61ac0-9208-5fb6-a0b8-665dd07bc44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350014} {:answer/id #uuid "aed5b553-32c7-51b5-8299-22eaac9710ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350015} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3040f8-b87d-5513-9f6d-1a235282a85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350016} {:answer/id #uuid "2423383e-2f02-5703-8028-24b7315f6bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350017} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb63271-39a8-53e5-8045-e9cb20281f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdb63271-39a8-53e5-8045-e9cb20281f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350018} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6cb7c60-a068-5852-9680-0fde2c16dac7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e68797a6-41ea-57f5-8a8b-ac4a6a6fe032" :question/math-academy-id "q-112632" :question/problem "Let $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where ${b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\  & ={c}_{2}+2{c}_{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})-2(-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a04de8f-3dfe-5d66-9d7b-bafab822ccf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350021} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d90e71-43ce-5c52-adc3-97d147851ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350022} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9dad9a-fa23-5dcb-aeaa-72fcd672d533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350023} {:answer/id #uuid "422ed304-e99c-5f65-ba96-4e650f5b5cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350024} {:answer/id #uuid "11d556fc-538a-595c-86cb-9a46c7991b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0d90e71-43ce-5c52-adc3-97d147851ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350022} :answer-field/id #uuid "5daa3e2b-1ebc-55ae-8bb0-56245e2b36d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3fda3c1-c4da-57dd-a60d-25f6952dc97b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112639" :question/problem "Let $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where ${b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=2{c}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+\\frac{1}{2}(2{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =9{b}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a2ea5d-11fe-5f05-a92b-e735867a1614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350028} {:answer/id #uuid "a84bc529-8a62-5ea2-920f-26a5ab131a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350029} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bf2ccc-d94d-55d0-a03b-d7348b27765d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350030} {:answer/id #uuid "c14f619e-95a7-5143-b23f-2ea9f31402ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350031} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8d7d27-b1f8-5880-bf73-f2c3be2ab6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bf2ccc-d94d-55d0-a03b-d7348b27765d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350030} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeedd3f3-f70d-5c4c-8e49-0600478c4f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0311d991-2f8f-5998-acfa-c7762bb90c0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112652" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}-{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-{b}_{3}+2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =5(-\\frac{1}{5}{b}_{3})+2\\cdot 2(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2})+5\\cdot 2(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =5{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}+10{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df96d21a-e1bc-5a9b-b528-d6e7b30d8451" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Three-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff5c31a-15cb-5e6e-bbfa-d344737e1cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350035} {:answer/id #uuid "397f0c6d-24ce-5c14-b9ca-b1011f8ea9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350036} {:answer/id #uuid "c851f7c7-8977-5d58-b43c-28d22ade8fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350037} {:answer/id #uuid "f1456cc1-3d15-5285-b76a-355285046b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350038} {:answer/id #uuid "d762b46a-0598-54a4-a12e-fc5a54a9b6eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397f0c6d-24ce-5c14-b9ca-b1011f8ea9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350036} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48ea519-aa13-5292-a7f7-8433bc45cd4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7835b8f5-b194-5486-8234-469b2eb98936" :question/math-academy-id "q-112810" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=-2{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-4{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-4{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3}+2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =4(-{b}_{2})+2{b}_{3}-(-2{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e568b6fc-88f0-592b-a24d-e3286cfa5043" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2adad20a-504e-5d34-b389-576567d86afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350042} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf65cc1-afa3-5a54-9df2-f2c375e18a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350043} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea2c2be-255b-5ea3-844b-c0942c90108f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350044} {:answer/id #uuid "fa32bfe0-9f0f-51ef-9dc2-eaa683e902c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350045} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7b0da2-7aed-5e46-aaa2-344f2582d8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adad20a-504e-5d34-b389-576567d86afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350042} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1fc2b8-045e-53b7-8101-56a310e1299b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1c7e82e-45cc-52c9-803f-d07a918acd1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112833" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-4{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=-4{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $5$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+5{c}_{2}=6{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-4(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2})+5{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{2}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2})-6(\\frac{2}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b27833-e930-5339-89b3-c5651d791dcd" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Two-Dimensional Vector in a Different Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2aa89a-42a3-5e7e-b8d1-62eaea424c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350048} {:answer/id #uuid "5963faf5-3383-5d48-8c43-a59b1d2f1d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350049} {:answer/id #uuid "efe05e06-b9e8-5dc3-b03c-114f9a19d30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350050} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebe9e03-5492-5203-a19d-4aded29c89ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350051} {:answer/id #uuid "94659304-3478-5a36-99db-c8fe9154aaed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value