swer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350111} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a84cd3-6902-5fa8-8815-3b37de2233e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350112} {:answer/id #uuid "abc40be0-fea0-59ac-8fc4-9dc040139697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350113} {:answer/id #uuid "07c026f8-b7ff-57c1-9e39-5d16077c9e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e835b54-72f7-588b-9809-0c71d06b9fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350111} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b6931e2-8f3c-5088-9e71-465016278699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13726c91-9509-5358-a245-a13ba912d2df" :question/math-academy-id "q-81229" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
